Title: THE CHRONICLES OF THE GUARDIANS
Episode: 107, "THE SUNDERING"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-07
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 15 script pages of a 50-minute episode. The bottle episode and centre of the season: Seraphine records the truth for the first time, alone, in the shop. It is staged to hold with the film on four points. (1) The Guardians built the Loom as a seat for themselves (film Act II: "We built a throne"), argued in secret under the Conclave's nose; the public line Ithren gives the Conclave in the film's prologue ("it was built to hold the stones apart") is the cover story. (2) The prologue's action is unchanged: Verran seats himself, the six pull stones, the sky tears, Ithren stuns Malachar, the six agree to the lie. (3) Film Act III: Malachar says the Conclave caused the Sundering and Eryndor answers "He pressed the button. We built it" and "Ithren knew." What he does not know, and the episode makes the audience know, is that the six decided the night before to shear the stones if Verran sat, knowing the shear would cost the city, and did not ask him because his daughter was in it. That is why his prologue line "Then we take them out" meets Ithren's stricken face. (4) The exact phrase "Hold the other end" is spoken once in the season, here, by Ithren; Malachar uses it in the film. Verran, Orsolya, Maevar, Tolun and Sabeth are founder-approved; the contingency vote, the sale of the drawings (who sold them is left open) and Eryndor keeping Liora's wrecked paper bird are proposals. Malachar's own paper bird is not in the flashbacks: the film has it lie in the dust and later in his pocket, and episode 103 has him leave one on the hillside stone. No contemporary plot beyond the frame. Auren is not in the episode and does not hear the recording (Locks 3, 4); Seraphine and Malachar do not meet in the present (Lock 2). The recording is later put on the high shelf Auren could not reach as a boy. Frequency motif: 9.1717 Hz. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. A BOOKSHOP UNDER A RAILWAY ARCH, PORT VESSA — NIGHT

The shop, shut. One green lamp. Seraphine at the counter in her cardigan with a battered cassette recorder in front of her, an old one, bought in a charity shop, and a microphone on a stand that belongs to a different decade. She has laid out a cup of tea she is not going to drink. Her hands are not quite steady.

Above the arch a late train goes over. The cup chatters on its saucer. The whole shop hums with it. And under the train, hardly there, a note: steady, dry, patient.

SERAPHINE
(to the ceiling, tired)
Yes. I know.

She presses RECORD. The tape begins to turn.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
(to the microphone, carefully)
For whoever holds the other end.

She stops. She lets the tape run on, blank, for a long breath.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT: SEVEN INSCRIBED, TWO LEFT EMPTY. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. THE BOOKSHOP — NIGHT

Seraphine, as before. The tape runs.

SERAPHINE
(to the recorder)
My name is Eryndor. I was a Guardian of the Aurelith. I am going to tell you what happened, and I am going to tell it in order, because I have told it out of order for four thousand years, and that is how a lie is kept.

A beat. She looks at the cup.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
First: what we said. We said the Conclave seized the Loom, and the Guardians stopped them at the price of the world. Every word of that is true. It is also the kind of truth that has had everything useful cut out.

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — UNDER CONSTRUCTION — DAY (FLASHBACK, YEARS BEFORE)

The same vault, half-built. Rings of raw metal stacked in cradles, scaffolds, lamps on hooks. Seven people stand round a table of drawings, in grey work-smocks, unremarkable, sleeves rolled. ITHREN, forty-six, steady. MALACHAR, thirty-six, kind hands, sleeves black with oil. ERYNDOR, spare, watchful, already the one who keeps the notes. And ORSOLYA, MAEVAR, TOLUN, SABETH.

On the table, a drawing of a seat. A broad plain chair, its arms ending in seven round hollows.

MALACHAR
(frowning at it)
Seven sockets. One seat.

ITHREN
A place to stand if the stones ever have to answer at once. Not a crown, Malachar. A governor on the whole machine.

MALACHAR
A seat has one occupant.

ORSOLYA
(gently)
It has the occupant we choose.

MALACHAR
And when we are dead?

A silence. Tolun, big, bearded, turns a spanner over in his hand.

TOLUN
Then somebody better, one hopes.

MALACHAR
(stubbornly)
Nobody is better. That's what the Conclave is for. If the stones answer to us and not to them —

ITHREN
(low, not unkind)
If the stones answer to the Conclave, they answer to whoever the Conclave has become by the time it matters.

SABETH
(quietly)
She's right.

A vote, with no ceremony. Six hands go up. Malachar looks at them, one at a time. Then he looks at the drawing.

MALACHAR
(a long breath)
Then I'll build it, because if it is going to exist it should be the safest thing in the world.

Eryndor writes a line in the margin of her notes and does not look up.

ERYNDOR (V.O.)
(over the scene, in the shop)
He built the finest lock ever made on the worst idea we ever had. And we told ourselves we were building a guardian for the world.

Ithren crosses to a ring, a great arm of raw metal in a cradle, and puts both hands to it. It will not turn.

ITHREN
Malachar. Hold the other end.

Malachar grins, wiped hands, quite suddenly very young, and takes the far side. Together they lean. The ring begins, with a long low note, to turn.

ERYNDOR (V.O.)
(softly)
That was the best day of my life.

ACT TWO

INT. THE BOOKSHOP — NIGHT

Seraphine presses PAUSE. A long silence. She looks at the green lamp. She presses RECORD again.

SERAPHINE
(to the recorder)
The Conclave's Speaker was a man called Verran. He was not a monster. He was sixty, and clever, and he had been told all his life that his office meant answering to nobody. He learned about the seat the way a man learns his house has a room he has not been shown.

INT. A COURTYARD IN THE AURELITH — MORNING (FLASHBACK, THE EVE)

A fountain. Washing, lanterns not yet lit. In an upstairs window, someone is singing.

On the edge of the fountain, a small girl of six, LIORA, sits with a square of paper and a ferocious frown, trying to fold a bird. She has done it wrong four times. Eryndor sits down beside her in her grey smock with a loaf under one arm.

LIORA
Aunt Ery, you hold it all wrong.

ERYNDOR
(rueful)
I hold everything wrong, my dear. Your father has the hands.

LIORA
(considering the paper)
Will you come to the festival?

ERYNDOR
(a beat; she does not look away)
I'll be at the Loom first. Then yes.

LIORA
Will it be loud?

ERYNDOR
(carefully)
Your papa says it's the quietest thing in the world.

LIORA
(satisfied)
He always says that.

Liora pushes the wrecked bird across the stone. Eryndor looks at it for a long time.

ERYNDOR (V.O.)
(very quietly)
I had the answer to her question the night before. I want you to know that.

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — NIGHT (FLASHBACK, THE NIGHT BEFORE)

Candles on a bench. Six Guardians sit in the dark vault. Not seven. A seventh place at the table is empty, a smock hung on the back of the chair.

ITHREN
(low)
Verran knows about the dais.

TOLUN
How?

ITHREN
Somebody sold the drawings. It does not matter who. Tomorrow, under the alignment, he is going to sit in it, and the stones are going to come to him. All seven. One man in one seat, and the world answers to whatever he wants for as long as he lives.

MAEVAR
(young, frightened)
We can refuse. We can break the Loom.

ITHREN
We cannot break it. We can only shear the stones free while it is drawing them. And a shear tears.

SABETH
(slowly)
Tears what?

ITHREN
(she has done the arithmetic; her voice is a thread)
Whatever stands closest to the rings.

ORSOLYA
(a whisper)
The city.

A very long silence. Orsolya begins, without a sound, to cry. Tolun puts his hand on her shoulder. He has nothing to say.

ERYNDOR
(carefully, to Ithren)
We should tell him.

ITHREN
He has a daughter in the city.

ERYNDOR
That is why we should tell him.

ITHREN
(a terrible gentleness)
He would stop it. He would walk up to that throne and take every stone out of the air with his own hands, and he would be the one who tore it. Or he would warn the Speaker, because he is the best of us and cannot lie. Either way the thing would be done by him, and I will not do that to him.

Eryndor opens her mouth. Closes it. She looks at the empty chair with the smock hung on it, and she nods.

ERYNDOR (V.O.)
(over the scene, in the shop)
Six hands. We did not vote this time. We did not need to.

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — THE DAY OF THE SUNDERING

The chamber as the film has it, the seven rings turning, the Conclave on the dais, Verran in his silver hair. Seven Guardians in grey. We do not see the whole scene again; we see it from Eryndor's position at the edge, her eyes on one man.

Verran lays a hand on the black throne. Malachar, a step from Ithren, says low, quick, urgent (the film's prologue exchange, compressed):

MALACHAR
We can still stop this. Then we take them out.

Ithren's face, turned to him, does something unbearable. It is a look we have not seen on her before and will see only once. She says, so quietly that only Eryndor hears it:

ITHREN
(a whisper)
You don't mean that.

ACT THREE

INT. THE BOOKSHOP — NIGHT

Seraphine presses STOP. The motor winds down and the cassette sits still. She presses RECORD once more. Her voice has gone very low and even.

SERAPHINE
(to the recorder)
When the stones came out of the air, the sky tore. I will not describe it. It is on every wall in every temple in the world, and none of them has it right. I will tell you only that I was looking at Malachar, and that he took a stone, too. He did what we had decided on without knowing we had decided it. He pulled his stone from the air a breath after the six of us. And then he turned, and he ran.

EXT. THE AURELITH — A RIDGE ROAD — THE SAME INSTANT

Malachar, running up a road against a river of people, his face wiped blank. The fountain is a stump. The terraces are dust.

SERAPHINE (V.O.)
He found a courtyard. I will not tell you what he found.

INT. THE LOOM CHAMBER — LATER (FLASHBACK)

Smoke and silence. The Loom dead, its rings frozen at odd angles. The six standing in a ring with stones in their hands. They are older than they were an hour ago. On the floor, the cracked throne, and beside it a body aged to bone in a robe.

ITHREN
(to the ring)
We were supposed to keep them apart.

ERYNDOR (V.O.)
(over it)
When he came back, Ithren laid him down. I have told you that part. I held his head.

A week later. A rough stone hall with no roof, three walls of the future Citadel standing, a long table of plain boards. The six at it, in the same grey, gaunter now. Orsolya's eyes are raw. Tolun is looking at nothing. At the head, Ithren.

ITHREN
(low)
We say it was Verran.

MAEVAR
(a whisper)
It was Verran.

ITHREN
It was Verran who sat. It was us who built the seat. I will not have the children of this world learn the second thing. A world that learns its saviours were its arsonists will never trust a saviour again.

SABETH
(turning away)
And Malachar?

ITHREN
(a very long beat)
When he wakes, we tell him what we told the world.

ERYNDOR
(almost inaudible)
And Liora?

ITHREN
(her voice has finally given out)
We tell him that too.

Nobody answers. Eryndor, at the end of the table, takes from her smock a small thing she took off the edge of a fountain on the morning of the festival: the wrecked paper bird a six-year-old folded four times wrong. Aurelith paper, that day, does not age. She looks at it for a long time. She does not say what she is thinking.

INT. THE BOOKSHOP — NIGHT

Seraphine, tape turning. On the counter in the lamplight lie two paper birds side by side: one small, clean, crooked at one wing, the one that was left on a hillside stone, and one creased to ruin by a child's four failures. She has her hand beside them and does not touch either.

SERAPHINE
(to the microphone, barely audible)
Ithren knew first. She wept for a week. Then she told us we must never say it. And she was right, and I have hated her for it every day since.

She lets the tape run. She is crying and has not noticed.

SERAPHINE (CONT'D)
He does not know. He must never know that we decided it the night before, in the dark, without him. That we paid for the sky with his daughter and called it a saving.

She presses STOP. The recorder clicks. Under the arch the next train comes, and the shop hums with it, and the cup chatters, and she sits very still.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE BOOKSHOP — NIGHT

The shop in the dark but for the green lamp. Seraphine on a chair, a tall one, reaching for the highest shelf with the cassette in a small tin. She has to stand on the seat. It is the same chair, we realise, as the one a boy once stood on in this room, trying to reach a book she had put too high.

She sets the two paper birds in the tin beside the cassette, closes it, and slides it onto the shelf behind the dusty row and steps down. She dusts her hands. She does not look at it again.

SERAPHINE
(to the empty shop, wry, exhausted)
If you can't get it down, who can?

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 107 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
